  <h2 id="certifications">CSCP, CPIM, and which certification actually matters</h2>
  <p>
    Not every certification carries the same weight in the Indian supply chain job market. Here is the
    honest picture of what actually moves the needle.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional):</strong> offered through ASCM/APICS, this is the most globally recognised SCM credential relevant to Indian employers with MNC or global exposure. It can raise pay by roughly 15-20% when it helps you change jobs, and certified professionals report meaningfully higher pay than uncertified peers. It works best once you already have some operational experience and are targeting a senior or globally-exposed move, not as a first step at 0-2 years.</li>
    <li><strong>CPIM (Certified in Planning and Inventory Management):</strong> a narrower, planning-focused ASCM credential, most useful if you are deliberately steering toward demand planning or inventory management rather than a generalist SCM career.</li>
    <li><strong>MBA / PGDM in Operations or Supply Chain Management:</strong> not required to enter SCM, but the strongest single accelerant toward manager-level roles, because it builds cross-functional business judgment beyond execution-level process knowledge.</li>
    <li><strong>SAP MM/WM or S/4HANA certification:</strong> increasingly valuable because most mid-size and large Indian companies run SCM operations through SAP or a comparable ERP. Practical, hands-on SAP skill often matters more to hiring managers than the certificate itself.</li>
    <li><strong>Six Sigma (Green Belt or Black Belt):</strong> useful in manufacturing-heavy supply chains where process improvement and defect reduction are core to the job, less relevant in e-commerce or pure logistics roles.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Whichever certification you consider, remember that interviewers for manager-level SCM roles test
    whether you have actually handled a real vendor negotiation, a real stockout, or a real delivery-cost
    crunch, not just whether you passed an exam. Treat certification as a credibility signal that opens
    the interview, not as a replacement for real, defensible operational experience.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The certifications and tool skills above only matter as part of a genuine high-value skill
    portfolio</strong> — operational judgment plus data fluency plus a credential, not a credential
    alone. That combination, not the SCM job title, is what unlocks the higher-paying manager and
    specialist tracks.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-growing">Why this field is genuinely growing, not overhyped</h2>
  <p>
    India's logistics cost has fallen to roughly 7.97% of GDP according to a joint 2025 study by the
    Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade (DPIIT) and the National Council of Applied
    Economic Research (NCAER), down from figures commonly cited around 13-14% of GDP a few years ago.
    The National Logistics Policy has been pushing this down toward a global-average benchmark, and that
    kind of structural cost reduction only happens when companies invest in better planning, better
    systems, and more supply chain talent, not less.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same underlying signals show up across independent market data: India's logistics market is
    projected to grow from roughly USD 349 billion in 2025 to USD 592 billion by 2031, and the
    warehousing market alone is expected to expand from about USD 14 billion in 2024 to USD 35 billion
    by 2029, a near-19% compound annual growth rate. E-commerce and quick-commerce companies, PLI-driven
    manufacturing expansion, and a growing 3PL sector are the practical hiring engines behind those
    numbers, not abstract policy targets.
  </p>
  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not a "safe forever" claim — no field gets that guarantee. But unlike some trending career
      terms that peak on social media and fade, supply chain demand is tied to physical goods movement
      and national infrastructure investment that does not disappear in a hiring-freeze cycle the way a
      single tech-hype role can. The honest risk is not "will SCM jobs exist" but "will you build the
      data and planning skill that separates a plateaued operational role from a genuinely growing one."
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="city-company">How city and company type change SCM pay</h2>
  <p>
    Two SCM professionals with the same title and years of experience can sit on genuinely different
    pay curves once you factor in where they work and what kind of company employs them. This is a
    subtopic most SCM-career guides skip entirely, and it changes real decisions about where to start.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bangalore, Delhi NCR (Gurgaon), Mumbai, and Pune</strong> pay meaningfully above the national average for the same SCM role, driven by e-commerce, quick-commerce, and GCC hiring density in these cities.</li>
    <li><strong>Global Capability Centres (GCCs)</strong> for SCM analytics, planning, and procurement roles increasingly pay 20-30% above a comparable domestic-company role, because these centres run supply chain strategy for large global businesses out of India.</li>
    <li><strong>PSU and government logistics roles</strong> (Indian Railways logistics wings, Food Corporation of India, state warehousing corporations) pay less at entry than a fast-growing private company, but offer far more job security and structured promotion timelines — a real option for people who value stability over the fastest possible pay climb.</li>
    <li><strong>Tier-2 city manufacturing hubs</strong> (parts of Gujarat, Tamil Nadu, and the NCR periphery) often pay less in headline number but come with a meaningfully lower cost of living, which can mean a comparable real standard of living to a higher headline salary in a metro.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    None of these is objectively "better" — the right choice depends on whether you are optimising for
    pay ceiling, stability, or cost-adjusted take-home at this stage of your career.
  </p>

