  <h2 id="roadmap">A realistic roadmap to your first SCM role</h2>
  <p>
    Here is what an honest plan looks like, whether you are still in college or considering a switch
    into supply chain. The exact pace depends on your starting point — some people move through the
    early stages in a focused stretch of months, others need a couple of years, and both are normal.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pick a starting lens early:</strong> decide whether procurement, logistics, inventory, or planning feels closest to work you would actually enjoy, since most people enter through one of these before branching further.</li>
    <li><strong>Build one real, checkable proof point now:</strong> a mock demand forecast using public sales data, a vendor cost-comparison sheet, or a basic inventory reorder-point calculation you can walk an interviewer through.</li>
    <li><strong>Get an internship inside your target lens, even unpaid or stipend-only:</strong> real exposure to a live warehouse floor, a real vendor negotiation, or a real logistics dashboard teaches judgment faster than most courses do.</li>
    <li><strong>Build practical Excel, SQL, and at least basic WMS or ERP fluency:</strong> this is the single most commonly requested skill set across entry-level SCM postings, and it is learnable without a formal degree.</li>
    <li><strong>Get outside validation before every real application:</strong> show your work to someone already working in supply chain or logistics and use their feedback to close gaps.</li>
    <li><strong>Add a matching certification once your foundation is solid:</strong> CPIM if you are steering toward planning, or CSCP once you have a couple of years of real operational experience and want broader credibility.</li>
  </ul>

